  "g7-history-c1-l1": {
    "id": "g7-history-c1-l1",
    "chapterId": "g7-history-c1",
    "title": "Cartography & Historical Terminology",
    "desc": "Al-Idrisi 1154 map vs Guillaume de L'Isle 1720 map, changing meaning of terms like 'Hindustan' and 'Foreigner'.",
    "content": "# Cartography & Historical Terminology\n\n## 1. Learning Objectives\n- Understand key historical/civic concepts of Cartography & Historical Terminology in Class 7.\n- Analyze primary historical texts, architectural styles, or state government structures.\n- Evaluate political formations, medieval religious synthesis, and democratic rights.\n- Master key terms, dynasties, and administrative systems for school exams.\n\n## 2. Introduction\nIn Class 7 History & Civics, **Cartography & Historical Terminology** examines medieval Indian history, architectural traditions, devotional movements, and state legislative governance. Al-Idrisi 1154 map vs Guillaume de L'Isle 1720 map, changing meaning of terms like 'Hindustan' and 'Foreigner'.\n\n## 3. Detailed Concept Explanation\nThe study of **Cartography & Historical Terminology** provides students with historical evidence literacy, architectural analysis skills, and civic understanding of democratic rights and state administration.\n\n### Key Historical & Civic Concepts\n1. **Historical Evidence & Dynasties:** Examining Prashastis, Chola bronze sculptures, Mughal mansabdari records, and 18th-century treaties.\n2. **Cultural & Architectural Evolution:** Analyzing Trabeate vs Arcuate architecture, Bhakti/Sufi devotional paths, and regional languages.\n3. **Democratic Institutions:** Understanding Universal Adult Suffrage, Article 15 equality provisions, and how MLAs form state governments.\n\nMastering **Cartography & Historical Terminology** requires analyzing historical sources, understanding administrative systems, and evaluating cause-and-effect relationships in history and civics.\n\n### Systematic Historical Inquiry Approach\n1. Identify the historical century, ruling dynasty, or civic institution.\n2. Examine primary texts (Akbarnama, Buranjis), inscriptions, or constitutional articles.\n3. Analyze socio-political transformations and economic systems.\n4. Draw verified historical and civic conclusions.\n\n## 4. Important Definitions\n- **Mansabdar:** A Mughal official who held a mansab (rank/position) determining salary, status, and military responsibilities.\n- **Prashasti:** A formal written eulogy composed by learned Brahmans in praise of a king.\n- **MLA (Member of Legislative Assembly):** An elected representative of a state legislative constituency in India.\n\n## 5. Key Concepts\n- Cartography and historical terms evolved significantly over the medieval thousand-year period (700–1750 CE).\n- Chola and Mughal administrative systems used sophisticated revenue collection (Zabt) and local self-governance.\n- Equality is a fundamental pillar of Indian democracy guaranteed under Article 15 of the Constitution.\n\n## 6. Examples\n### Example 1: Akbar's Policy of Sulh-i Kul\n*Solution:* Akbar established 'Universal Peace' (Sulh-i Kul) promoting honesty, justice, and peace across all religions.\n\n### Example 2: Chola Bronze Casting (Lost-Wax Technique)\n*Solution:* Sculptors created wax models, coated them with clay, melted the wax out, and poured molten bronze inside to create world-famous idols like Nataraja.\n\n### Example 3: Role of an MLA in Legislative Assembly\n*Solution:* MLAs debate public policies, ask questions to ministers, and vote on state budget bills inside the Vidhan Sabha.\n\n## 7. Step-by-Step Explanation\n### Historical & Civic Analytical Method\n1. State the historical period or civic issue.\n2. Examine primary records, monuments, or constitutional laws.\n3. Evaluate administrative structures and political impacts.\n4. Formulate verified historical conclusions.\n\n## 8. Formulas / Rules\n- **Chauth and Sardeshmukhi Tax Rule (Marathas):**\n  $$\\text{Chauth} = 25\\% \\text{ of Land Revenue}, \\quad \\text{Sardeshmukhi} = 9-10\\% \\text{ Additional Tax}$$\n- **State Legislative Majority:**\n  $$\\text{Majority Seats Required} > \\frac{\\text{Total Assembly Constituencies}}{2}$$\n\n## 9. Worked Problems / Activities\n### Worked Problem 1\nDifferentiate between Trabeate and Arcuate architectural styles.\n*Solution:* 1. Trabeate style roofs/doors were supported by placing horizontal beams across two vertical pillars. 2. Arcuate style used arches to carry the weight of the superstructure over doors/windows.\n\n### Worked Problem 2\nHow does a bill become a law in a State Legislative Assembly?\n*Solution:* 1. MLA introduces a bill. 2. Assembly debates and votes on the bill. 3. Passed bill is sent to the Governor for royal assent to become an Act.\n\n## 10. Try Yourself\n1. Name the Mughal revenue system introduced by Todar Mal based on 10-year crop yield survey.\n2. Which article of the Indian Constitution prohibits discrimination on grounds of religion, race, caste, sex, or place of birth?\n\n## 11. Common Mistakes\n- Confusing Jagirdars (received land revenue assignments) with Zamindars (local village chieftains).\n- Believing state laws are made by the Parliament (State laws are passed by the State Legislative Assembly!).\n\n## 12. Quick Revision & Key Takeaways\n- Cholas constructed Gangaikondacholapuram and famous lost-wax bronzes.\n- Akbar's Akbarnama was written by Abul Fazl.\n- Universal Adult Suffrage gives equal voting rights to every citizen aged 18 and above."
  },
